# ============================================================
# .gitignore for ASP.NET Core Web API
# ============================================================
# Git tracks your SOURCE CODE — the files YOU write.
# It should NOT track build outputs, secrets, or machine-specific
# files, because:
#   1. They are auto-generated (can always be recreated)
#   2. They can contain secrets (passwords, keys)
#   3. They are often huge (node_modules, bin, obj)
#   4. They differ per machine (IDE settings)
# ============================================================

# ---------------------------
# .NET Build Output
# ---------------------------
# 'bin' = compiled binaries (.dll, .exe). Generated by `dotnet build`.
# 'obj' = intermediate build files. Also auto-generated.
# You never need to commit these — anyone can run `dotnet build` to recreate them.
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# ---------------------------
# .NET User Secrets & Local Config
# ---------------------------
# These files hold sensitive local config (DB passwords, API keys).
# NEVER commit these to source control.
appsettings.*.local.json
secrets.json
*.user

# ---------------------------
# NuGet Packages
# ---------------------------
# NuGet packages are like npm packages — they're downloaded automatically
# via `dotnet restore`, so there's no need to commit them.
*.nupkg
*.snupkg
.nuget/
packages/
!**/[Pp]ackages/repositories.config

# ---------------------------
# Visual Studio / Rider IDE Files
# ---------------------------
# These are machine-specific IDE settings. Other developers on the team
# have their own IDE configs, so these shouldn't be shared via git.
.vs/
.idea/
*.suo
*.user
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates
*.rsuser

# ---------------------------
# VS Code
# ---------------------------
# Similarly, VS Code workspace settings are personal/machine-specific.
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json   # Exception: sharing recommended extensions IS useful

# ---------------------------
# OS Generated Files
# ---------------------------
# These are created automatically by your operating system. They have
# nothing to do with your code and clutter the repo.
.DS_Store         # macOS folder metadata
Thumbs.db         # Windows thumbnail cache
Desktop.ini       # Windows folder settings
*~                # Linux/macOS temp files

# ---------------------------
# Logs & Diagnostics
# ---------------------------
*.log
*.trace
logs/

# ---------------------------
# Publish Output
# ---------------------------
# `dotnet publish` outputs here. Like bin/obj, it's always regenerated.
[Pp]ublish/
PublishProfiles/

# ---------------------------
# Test Results
# ---------------------------
TestResults/
coverage/
*.coverage
*.coveragexml

# ---------------------------
# Docker
# ---------------------------
# If you use Docker, your .env file may contain secrets.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example    # Exception: a template .env file (with no real secrets) IS useful to share

tasks.md